We determine the plane tangent to the paraboloid z = x² + 3y² at the point (-2, 1, 7) in two ways: 

		1) by using formula 4 in 2.3, 

		2) by using our new formula 

First, the equation z = x² + 3y² explicitly describes the paraboloid as the graph of the function f(x, y) = x² + 3y²,  that is, by an equation of the form z = f(x, y). 
Therefore, formula 4 of 2.3 applies to tell us that tangent plane at (-2, 1, 7) has equation 

		z = f(-2, 1) + f(sub(x)) (-2, 1)(x + 2) + f(sub(y)) (-2, 1)(y - 1)

or, equivalently, 

		z = 7 - 4(x + 2) + 6(y - 1). 

Second, if we write the equation of the paraboloid as x² + 3y² - z = 0, then we see that it describes the paraboloid as the level set of height 0 of the three-variable function F(x, y, z) = x² + 3y² - z.
Hence, formula 6 applies and indicates that an eqauation for the tangent plane at (-2, 1, 7) is 

	F(sub(x)) (-2, 1, 7)(x + 2) + F(sub(y)) (-2, 1, 7)(y - 1) + F(sub(z)) (-2, 1, 7)(z - 7) = 0

or 

	-4(x + 2) + 6(y - 1) - 1(z - 7) = 0

