Example 8 may be viewed in a more general context. 
If S is the surface in R³ given by the equation z = f(x, y) (where f is differentiable), then formula 4 of 2.3 tells us that an equation for the plane tangent to S at the point (a, b, f(a, b)) is 

	z = f(a, b) + f(sub(x)) (a, b)(x - a) + f(sub(y)) (a, b)(y - b)

At the same time, the equation for S may be written as 

		f(x, y) - z = 0

Then, if we let F(x, y, z) = f(x, y) - z, we see that S is the level set of F at height 0. 
Hence, formula 6 tells us that the tangent plane at (a, b, f(a, b)) is 

